    chapter: "Chapter 6 — Alley",
    text: `Elias Marsh’s amended trial concluded three weeks after Wexler’s arrest, the jury returning a conviction on the first two fires after a deliberation considerably shorter than the length of the trial itself — the forensic evidence, stripped of the muddying third fire, apparently having presented a clearer picture than three weeks of testimony had managed to convey on its own.

June attended the reading of the verdict, sketching Marsh’s face one final time as the words were read aloud — not the haunted, ambiguous expression she’d rendered throughout the trial, but something quieter, more resigned, a man absorbing a consequence he had, by every account, actually earned. She felt none of the earlier uncertainty she’d carried while watching him under suspicion for a crime that, it turned out, had never been his.

Afterward, in the hallway, Marsh’s attorney — a weathered, soft-spoken woman named Dolores Kim who had fought the case with a tenacity June had come to respect regardless of her client’s guilt — stopped June near the elevators.

“I read about what happened to you,” Kim said. “The stalking. The alley.”

“It worked out,” June said simply.

“It uncovered evidence that should have been part of this trial from the beginning,” Kim said. “Whatever else is true about my client, he didn’t deserve to be convicted for a fire someone else set out of jealousy and ambition. You’re the reason that didn’t happen. I wanted you to know I understand that, even though the verdict today doesn’t change much for him overall.”

June considered the strange, complicated shape of the compliment — thanks for the outcome of a case she’d never intended to influence, offered by an attorney whose client remained, by every meaningful measure, guilty of real and serious harm. It was not a clean feeling. Very little about the entire ordeal, she was beginning to understand, had ever offered a clean feeling.

“I didn’t do it for him,” she said honestly. “I did it because someone was stalking me, and stopping that felt like the only sane response available.”

“Sometimes the sane response and the right one happen to overlap,” Kim said. “I’ll take it, either way.”`,
